    "['stp region-configuration', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'spanning-tree mst configuration', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'spanning-tree mst configuration', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nspanning-tree mst configuration\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\nThe Huawei `stp region-configuration` command enters the MST (Multiple Spanning Tree) region configuration mode, which is equivalent to Cisco's `spanning-tree mst configuration` command. Both are used to configure MST region parameters like instance-to-VLAN mappings.\", 'trans_template': 'spanning-tree mst configuration'}]]": 1,
    "['instance [parameter1] vlan [parameter2] [parameter3] to [parameter4]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'spanning-tree mst configuration', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'spanning-tree mst configuration', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nspanning-tree mst configuration\\ninstance 2 vlan 20,300-400\\n##', 'trans_template': 'spanning-tree mst configuration'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'instance 2 vlan 20,300-400', 'parent_command': ['spanning-tree mst configuration'], 'root': 'spanning-tree mst configuration', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nspanning-tree mst configuration\\ninstance 2 vlan 20,300-400\\n##', 'trans_template': 'instance 2 vlan 20,300-400'}]]": 1,
    "['erps ring [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'erps enable', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'erps enable', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nerps enable\\nerps domain 2\\n##\\n\\nNote: Cisco\\'s ERPS (Ethernet Ring Protection Switching) implementation is different from Huawei\\'s. In Cisco, you would typically enable ERPS globally and then configure the domain/ring parameters. The exact equivalent doesn\\'t exist, but this represents the closest functional match to \"erps ring 2\" in Cisco\\'s terminology. Cisco\\'s ERPS configuration would require additional parameters to match Huawei\\'s complete ring configuration.', 'trans_template': 'erps enable'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'erps domain 2', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'erps domain 2', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nerps enable\\nerps domain 2\\n##\\n\\nNote: Cisco\\'s ERPS (Ethernet Ring Protection Switching) implementation is different from Huawei\\'s. In Cisco, you would typically enable ERPS globally and then configure the domain/ring parameters. The exact equivalent doesn\\'t exist, but this represents the closest functional match to \"erps ring 2\" in Cisco\\'s terminology. Cisco\\'s ERPS configuration would require additional parameters to match Huawei\\'s complete ring configuration.', 'trans_template': 'erps domain 2'}]]": 1,
    "['control-vlan [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'erps control-vlan 20', 'parent_command': ['erps enable'], 'root': 'erps enable', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nerps control-vlan 20\\n##\\n\\nNote: Cisco's ERPS (Ethernet Ring Protection Switching) implementation may differ slightly from Huawei's, and the exact command syntax might vary depending on the Cisco platform and IOS version. The above translation assumes a Cisco device supporting ERPS with similar configuration parameters. In some Cisco implementations, the command might be `erps ring control-vlan 20` or similar. Always verify with Cisco's documentation for your specific platform.\", 'trans_template': 'erps control-vlan 20'}]]": 1,
    "['protected-instance [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'erps instance 2 protected-vlan 20,300-400', 'parent_command': ['erps enable'], 'root': 'erps enable', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nerps instance 2 protected-vlan 20,300-400\\n##\\n\\nNote: Since Cisco doesn't have a direct 1:1 equivalent for Huawei's ERPS (Ethernet Ring Protection Switching) implementation, this is the closest approximation. The Cisco command would need to:\\n1. Map the protected-instance to an ERPS instance\\n2. Explicitly list the VLANs (rather than referencing an MST instance)\\n3. The VLAN range format differs slightly (using comma and hyphen)\\n\\nThe actual implementation might require additional Cisco-specific ERPS configuration depending on the platform and IOS version.\", 'trans_template': 'erps instance 2 protected-vlan 20,300-400'}]]": 1,
    "['wtr-timer [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'erps wtr-timer 6', 'parent_command': ['erps enable'], 'root': 'erps enable', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nerps wtr-timer 6\\n##\\n\\nNote: Cisco's ERPS (Ethernet Ring Protection Switching) implementation may use slightly different syntax, but this is the closest equivalent to Huawei's `wtr-timer` command. The value (6 minutes) remains the same. Some Cisco platforms might implement similar functionality under G.8032 configuration if they don't support ERPS directly.\", 'trans_template': 'erps wtr-timer 6'}]]": 1,
    "['guard-timer [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'erps timer guard-time 100', 'parent_command': ['erps enable'], 'root': 'erps enable', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nerps timer guard-time 100\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Cisco's implementation of ERPS (Ethernet Ring Protection Switching), the equivalent command for HUAWEI's `guard-timer` is `erps timer guard-time` with the value in centiseconds (same as HUAWEI's implementation).\\n- The value 100 remains unchanged as both vendors use centiseconds for this timer.\\n- This command sets the time the ring nodes wait before transitioning ports after topology changes.\", 'trans_template': 'erps timer guard-time 100'}]]": 1,
    "['version [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'erps version 2', 'parent_command': ['erps enable'], 'root': 'erps enable', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nerps version 2\\n##\\n\\nNote: Cisco's ERPS (Ethernet Ring Protection Switching) implementation may differ from Huawei's, and the exact command syntax might vary depending on the Cisco platform and IOS version. The above translation assumes a Cisco device supporting ERPS with similar configuration options. In some Cisco implementations, this might also be configured under a specific ring instance or interface context.\", 'trans_template': 'erps version 2'}]]": 1,
    "['sub-ring', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'revertive', 'parent_command': ['erps enable'], 'root': 'erps enable', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"For translating the HUAWEI `sub-ring` ERPS (Ethernet Ring Protection Switching) configuration to Cisco's G.8032 (Ethernet Ring Protection) equivalent, the corresponding command would be `role secondary`. \\n\\n##\\nrevertive\\nrole secondary\\n##\\n\\n### Explanation:\\n1. In HUAWEI's ERPS, `sub-ring` designates the ring as a secondary/sub-ring in a multi-ring topology.\\n2. In Cisco's G.8032, `role secondary` serves the same purpose for a ring instance.\\n3. `revertive` is added as it's typically enabled by default in ERPS/G.8032 for automatic traffic reversion after recovery.\\n\\nNote: Cisco's G.8032 implementation requires additional context (like ring-id, instance, etc.), but this is the direct functional equivalent of the `sub-ring` command.\", 'trans_template': 'revertive'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'role secondary', 'parent_command': ['erps enable'], 'root': 'erps enable', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"For translating the HUAWEI `sub-ring` ERPS (Ethernet Ring Protection Switching) configuration to Cisco's G.8032 (Ethernet Ring Protection) equivalent, the corresponding command would be `role secondary`. \\n\\n##\\nrevertive\\nrole secondary\\n##\\n\\n### Explanation:\\n1. In HUAWEI's ERPS, `sub-ring` designates the ring as a secondary/sub-ring in a multi-ring topology.\\n2. In Cisco's G.8032, `role secondary` serves the same purpose for a ring instance.\\n3. `revertive` is added as it's typically enabled by default in ERPS/G.8032 for automatic traffic reversion after recovery.\\n\\nNote: Cisco's G.8032 implementation requires additional context (like ring-id, instance, etc.), but this is the direct functional equivalent of the `sub-ring` command.\", 'trans_template': 'role secondary'}]]": 1,
