    "['stp disable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'spanning-tree portfast trunk', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nspanning-tree portfast trunk\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Cisco devices, `stp disable` on an interface is typically replaced with `spanning-tree portfast trunk` for trunk ports. This command effectively disables STP (Spanning Tree Protocol) on the interface by putting it into a forwarding state immediately, similar to the Huawei `stp disable` command.\\n- The `trunk` keyword is added since this is a trunk port configuration in the original Huawei setup.', 'trans_template': 'spanning-tree portfast trunk'}]]": 1,
    "['erps ring [parameter1] rpl owner', [{'para_map': [0, 0], 'trans_command': 'ptp clock-domain [parameter1]', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]'}]]": 1,
    "['stp disable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'spanning-tree portfast trunk', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nspanning-tree portfast trunk\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Cisco devices, `stp disable` on an interface is typically replaced with `spanning-tree portfast trunk` for trunk ports. This command:\\n  1. Immediately transitions the port to forwarding state (bypassing STP listening/learning states)\\n  2. Is specifically for trunk ports (matching the Huawei configuration)\\n  3. Achieves the same effect as disabling STP on the interface in Huawei's implementation\\n\\nNote: For production networks, you should ensure this is the desired behavior, as disabling STP can create risks of switching loops if not properly managed.\", 'trans_template': 'spanning-tree portfast trunk'}]]": 1,
    "['erps ring [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'erps enable', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nerps enable\\nerps domain 2\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Cisco's equivalent to Huawei's ERPS (Ethernet Ring Protection Switching) is typically configured using G.8032 (Ethernet Ring Protection) or REP (Resilient Ethernet Protocol), but exact command mapping isn't direct.\\n2. The above translation assumes basic ERPS ring activation in Cisco's context. For full functional equivalence, additional Cisco-specific parameters would need to be configured to match Huawei's control-vlan, protected-instance, and timer settings.\\n3. For precise Cisco implementation, you would need to use either:\\n   - `erps enable` + `erps domain` (for ERPS standard implementation)\\n   - Or Cisco's proprietary `rep segment 2` (for REP protocol)\", 'trans_template': 'erps enable'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'erps domain 2', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nerps enable\\nerps domain 2\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Cisco's equivalent to Huawei's ERPS (Ethernet Ring Protection Switching) is typically configured using G.8032 (Ethernet Ring Protection) or REP (Resilient Ethernet Protocol), but exact command mapping isn't direct.\\n2. The above translation assumes basic ERPS ring activation in Cisco's context. For full functional equivalence, additional Cisco-specific parameters would need to be configured to match Huawei's control-vlan, protected-instance, and timer settings.\\n3. For precise Cisco implementation, you would need to use either:\\n   - `erps enable` + `erps domain` (for ERPS standard implementation)\\n   - Or Cisco's proprietary `rep segment 2` (for REP protocol)\", 'trans_template': 'erps domain 2'}]]": 1,
